<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dengizich</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 469</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dengizich was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> who tried to restore Hun power after the empire broke up following his father's death in 453 and the defeat at the Nedao in 454. He campaigned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> and in the late 460s attacked the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. He was killed in 469 by the Roman general Anagastes, and his head was displayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>'s death his empire was divided among his three sons, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellac">Ellac</a>, Dengizich and Ernak. When Ellac died at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nedao">Battle of Nedao</a> in 454, Dengizich succeeded him, probably sharing the kingship with Ernak, each ruling separate peoples in separate lands. The name is spelt in several ways by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/priscus">Priscus</a>, Marcellinus Comes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a>; Otto Maenchen-Helfen derived it from a Turkic word meaning little lake, while Omeljan Pritsak read it as ocean-like.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a>, the sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> soon attacked Valamir, king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>, treating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> as runaway slaves, and were so badly beaten that the survivors fled to the lands by the river Danaber, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> called Var. In 465 or 466, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/priscus">Priscus</a> records, Dengizich and Ernak sent envoys to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> to ask for a peace treaty and a market on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, but were refused. Dengizich then moved to the lower Danube and threatened to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> unless he was given land and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a>. He refused to negotiate with Anagastes, who defended the Danube in Thrace, and sent envoys directly to Emperor Leo I, who answered that he would do everything if the Huns came and offered obedience.</p><p>In 467 Dengizich crossed the frozen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. Roman armies under Basiliscus, Anagastes, Ostryis and the Hun Chelchal trapped a force of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> in a valley, where Chelchal told the Goths that land would be given only for the Huns among them; the Goths turned on the Huns, and in the fighting that followed the survivors broke through the Roman lines. In about 468, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a> reports, Dengizich gathered the few peoples still under him, the Ultzinzures, Angisciri, Bittugures and Bardores, and besieged Bassianae in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>, but the Goths drove him off.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The war ended in 469. According to the Chronicon Paschale, his head was carried in procession through the Middle Street of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and set on a pole at the Wooden Circus, and the whole city turned out to see it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dengizich” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Dengizich</b> attacked the Romans in 467 without his brother Ernak, was besieged, and was defeated by the general Aspar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hunnic-empire">Hunnic Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
